# Games Plan Documents — Sync Rule

Two planning projects exist as documentation only. When implementation begins, or when
any new information changes an assumption, **the documents must be updated in the same
piece of work as the code.**

| Folder | Covers |
|---|---|
| `New games plan/` | Stage 0 defect fixes, then the in-house game-module architecture |
| `External game plans/` | Integrating games from a third-party skill-game provider |

## Two scenarios, and which chapters apply

`External game plans/` covers **two** decisions. Check `External game plans/PROGRESS.md`
for which one is on record before planning any work.

| Scenario | Meaning | Chapters | Programme |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Add-on** | The `New games plan` is delivered too. External games are an addition | `01`-`09` | E1-E9, 7.5-8 weeks engineering on top of the foundation |
| **External-only** | Provider games are the **only** new games. No in-house game | `01`-`09` **and** `10`-`18` | X0-X12, 20-26 weeks total |

Chapters `10`-`18` exist because `01`-`09` delegated the foundation and every
platform-wide change (admin, navigation, wording, flags, catalogue, risks, rollout) to the
`New games plan`. If that plan is not being delivered, the dependency does not disappear -
it moves into `External game plans/`, and `10`-`18` own it.

**The finding to keep repeating, because the intuition is wrong:** external-only is **not
cheaper**, it is broader. Dropping an in-house game saves ~3.5 weeks; a correct provider
integration costs ~7; all the platform work is identical. The prize is a catalogue and no
content burden, not a saving. Do not let a summary anywhere imply otherwise.

**Stage 0 is a prerequisite in both scenarios.** It fixes live defects under the money
layer, and nothing changes that.

**Stage 0 started on 1 September 2026** and is tracked in `New games plan/PROGRESS.md`,
which is the file to read before touching any of this. Three things from it are easy to
get wrong and are worth stating here:

- **Prerequisite A already shipped** (commit `d5d3a328`): the `/api/simulator/*` routes
  accepted a plain `X-Simulator-Mode: true` header as authentication in production, so an
  unauthenticated caller could credit any wallet. Recorded as **R27** in
  `External game plans/17-risk-register.md` because the provider callback specified in
  `01`/`06` can repeat the same mistake. Two rules follow: **an authentication helper must
  never accept a request because configuration is missing**, and **test the route, not just
  the helper.**
- **The finalize-time prize-pool safeguard does not mask the join-path defect.** It only
 fires when the pool is too *high*. An under-counted pool is under-distributed silently.
 Several documents used to claim the opposite; if you find that claim, it is stale.
- **Model mirror drift is a WRITE-side defect, with one read-side trap.** Measured against
 a real MongoDB on 1 Sep 2026 (`__tests__/helpers/mirror-drift-behaviour.test.ts`, 8
 tests). An ordinary `save()` does **not** strip fields the schema lacks, and `.lean()` /
 `toObject()` do **not** hide them — several documents used to claim both, and both are
 stale. What actually happens: a **missing enum value rejects the whole write**, and the
 app with the narrower copy **cannot write the field at all**, silently, while reporting
 success. **The exception, and the subtlest part: ordinary `doc.field` access returns
 `undefined`**, because Mongoose defines getters only for declared paths — so the field
 survives a debug dump while the code beside it reads nothing and takes the wrong branch.
 Two rules follow: **compare enum values, not just field names**, and **never cite a read
 as evidence that two copies agree.**
- **Defect 2 is built** (1 Sep 2026, awaiting owner test): the guard in
 `tools/model-mirror/`, all 11 drifted pairs synced, 20 tests, and the
 `mongodb-memory-server` replica-set harness the money tests need. It found **three** live
 production bugs on the way — failed withdrawals recording no time or reason, six
 landing-page sections that could not be edited at all, and hero/branding images that
 could never be restored after a redeploy. The third was found by **diffing the typecheck
 against a pre-sync baseline** (admin app 229 → 225 errors); errors that *disappear* after
 a sync mark code that was already reaching for a field its schema denied it. Worth
 repeating whenever models are synced.
- **The order is Defect 2 before Defect 1**, so the mirror guard exists before Defect 1
 edits the admin copy of the entry path.
- **Three owner decisions were taken on 1 Sep 2026** and must not be quietly reversed:
 the test database is **`mongodb-memory-server` as a single-node replica set** (built and
 proven with a real transaction); the orphaned declaration files are **deleted**; and
 **joining a competition is allowed outside market hours — only trading itself is gated.**
 That last one changes the Defect 1 design: the unified `contest-entry.service.ts` takes
 the union of the *security* checks from both gates and deliberately does **not** carry
 Gate B's market-hours check, because doing so would block weekend sign-ups for Monday
 contests. Locked in by Defect 1 test 12, which fails both a fix that blocks the join and
 one that lets the order through.
- **`apps/admin` has its own `node_modules/mongoose`.** Admin models therefore run on a
 **separate Mongoose instance** which a test's `mongoose.connect` does not touch. The
 symptom is `Operation "x.insertOne()" buffering timed out after 10000ms`, which reads like
 a slow database rather than an unconnected one. Connect it via **`Model.base`**, not a
 guessed path — that is the instance the model registered on, and it survives npm hoisting.
 Second rule from the same area: **never import both copies of one model into a single
 test.** Both register under the same name via `models.X || model(...)`, so the second
 import silently returns the first and the test examines the wrong schema while looking
 correct.
- **The `brandingFiles` image-recovery bug is NOT fixed by the model sync** (found 1 Sep
 2026, `__tests__/services/mirror-sync-behaviour.test.ts`). The upload route uses the
 filename as the map key and **Mongoose refuses map keys containing a dot**. The sync
 changed the failure mode without removing it: before, the undeclared field meant a plain
 JavaScript `Map` accepted the key and the write was silently discarded; after, `default:
 new Map()` hands the route a `MongooseMap` that validates and throws, and line 108 of
 `apps/admin/app/api/hero-settings/upload/route.ts` catches it and warns. **No entry has
 ever been stored, so there is nothing to migrate when it is fixed.** If a document claims
 the sync fixed hero/branding image recovery, that claim is stale.
- **Classify concurrency failures, never merely count them.** Asserting that the artifact
 and unknown buckets are *empty* is what stops test-server noise inflating a finding. And
 note the trap in the other direction: a genuine write conflict reads `Write conflict during
 plan execution **and yielding is disabled**`, so matching "yielding is disabled" as an
 infrastructure marker files every real failure as noise and makes the defect vanish.
- **`ensureCollections()` alone is not enough — indexes are a catalog change too.**
 Mongoose builds a model's indexes on first use, which produces the same `due to catalog
 changes` failure inside a transaction. That residue made the sequential control test pass
 alone and fail about **one full-suite run in three**. `settleIndexes()` awaits
 `Model.init()` for every registered model and `ensureCollections()` calls it. A test that
 fails only when the suite is busy teaches the team to rerun rather than to read.
- **Before writing any concurrency test, call `ensureCollections()`** from
 `__tests__/helpers/mongo-test-server.ts` with every collection the code under test writes.
 MongoDB **cannot create a collection inside a transaction**, so the first test to touch a
 new collection fails with `due to catalog changes` — which is indistinguishable at a glance
 from a contention failure. This nearly put a false finding on record: a 20-way join test
 reported 1 success in 20, and most of the failures were this artifact. **Measured after the
 fix, Gate A really does admit only 1 in 20 concurrent joins** (`Write conflict`, no retry,
 while Gate B retries five times) — but the ratio is not a production prediction, because a
 single-node in-memory replica set has tighter lock timeouts. Assert against the *observed*
 success count, never against the attempt count, so the test survives the fix.
- **Test fixtures must use ObjectId-shaped user ids** (24 hex chars), not readable labels.
 Better Auth uses the MongoDB adapter so real ids are ObjectId strings; most models type
 `userId` as `String` and would accept anything, but the fraud models declare
 `Schema.Types.ObjectId` and throw a `CastError` that the entry path catches and logs. The
 test then passes while a whole branch of the code under test never runs — coverage that
 only looks like coverage.
- **Testing a server action needs `__tests__/helpers/server-action-context.ts`**, which
 supplies the session, headers, restriction and fraud outcomes that ambient request state
 would normally provide. Two things will waste your time otherwise. Server actions
 **return** `{ success: false, error }` rather than throwing, so assert on the result, not
 with `rejects.toThrow`. And they re-throw only errors whose **`digest` starts with
 `NEXT_`** — a `redirect()` mock without a digest is swallowed into a result object, the
 redirect appears never to fire, and **the production code looks broken when it is
 correct.** Every guard test must assert the refusal left **no participant, no debit and no
 prize-pool change**, not merely the right message.
- **Defect 1's entry-fee ledger defect is proven, not inferred** (1 Sep 2026,
 `__tests__/services/entry-fee-ledger.test.ts`). Gate A writes `referenceId`, which the
 `WalletTransaction` schema does not declare, and never sets `competitionId`, which it
 does — so **every competition entry fee is unattributable to its competition.** Two
 things to state accurately: the harm is a **broken audit trail, not a wrong balance**
 (nothing computes money from that field — checked), and because *both* copies write
 `referenceId` identically this is **one bug duplicated, not mirror drift**, so the guard
 does not catch it and cannot.

## The rule

**Documentation is part of the deliverable, not a follow-up task.** A phase is not
finished until the documents match what was actually built.

On every change during implementation:

1. **Update `PROGRESS.md`** in the relevant folder — status table, work log entry,
   decisions, open questions. This is the file a new chat reads first.
2. **Update the affected chapter(s).** If the data model changed, `04-data-model.md`
   changes. If a flow changed, the flow document changes.
3. **Update the HTML versions**, which are generated from the same content and drift
   silently otherwise:
   - `New games plan/ChartVolt-New-Games-Plan.html` — internal
   - `New games plan/ChartVolt-New-Games-Plan-Partners.html` — partner-facing
   - `External game plans/ChartVolt-External-Games-Plan.html` — internal
   - `External game plans/ChartVolt-Game-API-Requirements.html` — sent to providers
4. **Record deviations explicitly.** If the build differed from the plan, say so in the
   work log with the reason. Never silently rewrite the plan to match the code.
5. **Check whether docs outside these folders are affected** — `README.md`,
   `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`, `deploy/README.md`, the admin wiki
   (`apps/admin/components/admin/AdminWikiSection.tsx`), and
   `legal/ChartVolt-Regulatory-Defence-Pack.html` if anything touches the
   skill-versus-chance argument or the money flow.

## Paired documents that must never drift

| Pair | Constraint |
|---|---|
| `01-provider-contract-specification.md` and `ChartVolt-Game-API-Requirements.html` | Same requirements, different audience. A provider building against a stale spec is worse than no spec. The HTML carries a **version number and a document-history table** — bump both whenever `01` changes, since providers may already be building against the issued version |
| `External game plans/10-external-only-programme.md` section 3, `PROGRESS.md`'s X-phase table, and section 15 of `ChartVolt-External-Games-Plan.html` | The X0–X12 phase list, effort figures and the 18–24 week total appear in all three. Changing one means changing all three |
| `External game plans/11`–`18` and their `New games plan` sources (`01`, `02`, `07`, `08`, `09`, `11`, `12`, `13`, `15`) | The external chapters restate load-bearing facts — line numbers, the five finalization entry points, the eight missing RBAC IDs — rather than duplicating whole chapters. **If a fact changes in the source, change it in the restatement too**, or the two plans will disagree about the codebase |
| `14-implementation-phases.md`, `00-README.md`, `PROGRESS.md` and the roadmap sections of both New-Games-Plan HTMLs | Phase list, effort estimates and totals appear in five places. Changing one means changing all five. Currently: Stage 0 (**6–9 days**), P1–P7 (**14–19 weeks**), plus the unscheduled per-game marketplace. The partner HTML's timeline SVG is drawn to scale — rescale the bars, not just the labels. Note the partner HTML must not carry the Stage 0 figure at all |
| The mirror-drift counts, wherever they appear | `New games plan` `00-README.md`, `12-risk-register.md`, `00a`, `03-data-model-changes.md`; `External game plans` `17-risk-register.md`, `18-migration-testing-rollout.md`; both internal HTMLs. Measured 1 Sep 2026 by the guard itself: **75 mirrored model pairs, 11 drifted** (all now synced), plus 19 action and 51 service files and **31 stale committed `.d.ts` files**. Earlier documents said "~21 files, 3 drifted", or "10 drifted" — both are stale. Do not hand-count these; run `npm run check:mirrors:list` |
| `External game plans/19-game-masters.md`, `New games plan/15` section 5, and the Game Master sections of the internal HTMLs | The Game Master system is the same system in both scenarios. `19` sections 1–4 are the authoritative analysis; `New games plan/15` section 5 restates it. **A fact corrected in one must be corrected in the other** |
| `ChartVolt-New-Games-Plan.html` and `-Partners.html` | The partner version must **never** mention Stage 0, the duplicate join paths, the admin mirror drift, or any current defect |
| Any model change | Exists twice: `database/models/` and `apps/admin/database/models/`. Update both in the same commit. **`npm run check:mirrors` now enforces this** in CI and on `git push`, comparing field paths *and* enum values. Do not "fix" a failure by deleting an enum value to make two sides agree — that orphans every document already storing it. Add to the side that lacks it. Deliberate differences go in `tools/model-mirror/allowlist.ts` **with a reason** |
| Committed compiled `.js` files under `database/` and `lib/` | **All 113 deleted 1 Sep 2026** (57 `.js` + 56 `.js.map`), owner approved. A *separate, earlier* accident from the `.d.ts` one — emitted 22 Dec 2025, same 57 modules, and **six to seven months stale**. Critically, **these were NOT inert**: vitest resolved the stale `.js` in preference to the `.ts`, which made every model and every money-critical service **unimportable in tests** and blocked the whole of Defect 1. `tsx`, esbuild and `next build` all resolve the `.ts` correctly, so production was never affected — which is why it survived unnoticed. Two rules follow: **when deleting emitted output, look for the other half of the emit** (a `tsc` run that produced `.d.ts` produced `.js` too — the first cleanup removed one half and left the harmful one), and **prove which file a resolver picks rather than reasoning about it** — a missing export makes a perfect probe. The `.gitignore` rule is scoped to `/database/**` and `/lib/**`, never a blanket `*.js`, because `ecosystem.config.js`, `scripts/*.js`, `worker/build.mjs` and the `api-server` build are hand-written |
| Committed `.d.ts` files | **All 112 deleted 1 Sep 2026** (57 `.d.ts` + 55 `.d.ts.map`), owner approved, with a `.gitignore` rule. They were orphaned February 2026 build output, stale, and **inert** — TypeScript resolves the sibling `.ts` first; proven by identical typechecks before and after (16 main, 225 admin). Earlier documents said "two", then "31 + 31" — the 31 counted only `database/` and missed 26 files under `lib/`. **Never treat a `.d.ts` as a third copy of a model to maintain.** Two files are deliberately kept and must stay tracked: `types/global.d.ts` (hand-written, no sibling `.ts`) and `websocket-server/dist/index.d.ts` (separate build). The rule for any future cleanup: delete a `.d.ts` only if a sibling `.ts` exists and it is not under `dist/` |

## Direction of travel (owner, 30 Aug 2026)

The platform is becoming **games-first**: players browse games, open a game page with
its description and rules, and join a competition from there. Trading becomes one game
among several — **demoted in navigation, not degraded in function.**

Recorded in `New games plan/15-platform-transformation-and-gaps.md`, which also holds
the three things chapters `01`–`14` did not cover: the games catalogue as admin-managed
content, the per-game marketplace, and Game Master residuals.

## Hard constraints recorded in the plans

Do not quietly reverse these; they are load-bearing. If one must change, update the
decision log in the relevant `PROGRESS.md` and say why.

- An external provider **never touches money** — no wallet access, no balances, no payouts.
- Only **skill-based** games are in scope. Chance-determined outcomes invert the
  regulatory position in `legal/ChartVolt-Regulatory-Defence-Pack.html`.
- **Stage 0 first.** `New games plan/00a-STAGE-0-prerequisite-fixes-DO-FIRST.md` is a
  prerequisite with its own owner sign-off, before any games work starts.
- `gameKey` is **immutable** once written — it is the join key for all historical stats.
- Scores enter the system through **exactly one** ingestion function.
- **Nothing in the marketplace may improve a player's score or ranking in a paid
  contest.** Cosmetics, titles, entry-fee vouchers and extra *practice* are fine;
  extra time, retries, hints, skips and easier content are not. Fairness, the
  regulatory position and chargeback exposure each independently require this.
- **No random or mystery packs**, even cosmetic ones — paying for a randomised reward
  introduces the chance element the regulatory defence depends on being absent.
- The **game module registry (code)** and the **game catalogue entry (content)** are
  separate concepts. One module may back several catalogue entries.
- Build the games catalogue and games-first navigation **after** the first real second
  game exists. An almost-empty catalogue is worse than none. It is **P7**, last.
- **Page content per game is contractual for external providers** — tagline,
  description, rules summary, how-to-play, thumbnail and banner, localised into every
  locale they declare, with no provider branding in the copy. Each provider title gets
  its own game page; a provider who will not supply content is transferring their
  content cost to us per title, forever.
- `/competitions` is **retained** alongside `/games`. Some players browse by game,
  others by what starts soonest or has the biggest pot. Do not replace one with the other.
- **No paid format is ever single-player.** A **competition** is two or more players,
  usually many, with winners sharing the pool. A **challenge** is exactly two. Practice is
  the only one-player mode and it is free, unranked and prize-less. `minParticipants`
  defaults to 2 and auto-cancels with full refunds below it — that behaviour already exists
  and must not be weakened.
- **"Independent play" describes gameplay, not contest size**, and the earlier name
  "solo-scored" was replaced because it was read as the latter. Trading is itself an
  independent-play game: every trader plays their own account and all are ranked together.
  If a document implies an independent-play game means a solo contest, that is a bug.
- Use **"challenge"**, never "duel". The codebase has a `Challenge` model, `/challenges`
  routes, `challengesEnabled`, and `challenge_entry` / `challenge_refund` ledger values.
  The provider-facing capability flag is `supportsOneVsOne`.
- **Game Masters must work identically on every game** — create contests, refer players,
  earn a share of referred players' entry fees. They are **~2.5 weeks of work, not four
  days**; any document saying otherwise is stale. Two facts that are easy to get wrong:
  a Game Master earns from **referred players' entry fees in any contest**, not from
  contests they created; and the Game Master competition route **inserts with the raw
  MongoDB driver and sets no game label**, which makes stamping it a **gate** in the
  foundation phase rather than a task.
- **A Game Master may not create external-provider contests until the revenue share is
  computed on net platform fee after provider cost.** `limits.allowedGameTypes` defaults
  to `["trading"]`. They still *earn* from provider contests from day one. Reason: the
  share is a percentage of the entry fee taken before provider cost exists, and the
  existing cap is against **gross** platform fee, so a low-fee contest can be net
  loss-making while still paying out. `External game plans/19-game-masters.md` section 5.
